    "From as early as the 18th and 19th centuries many Pontic Greeks had crossed into the Caucasus and southern Russia, where they found work in the mines and on the land; Georgia, Krasnodar and Stavropol had large Greek villages. Under Stalin their fate turned harsh: in 1937–38, in the so-called Greek Operation of the NKVD, thousands of Greeks were arrested and shot, and in 1949 more than 37,000 Greeks were deported from the Black Sea coast to Central Asia, mostly to southern Kazakhstan around Shymkent. Greek schools and newspapers were closed, and the Pontic dialect survived mainly at home.",
