    "When the Soviet Union broke up, tens of thousands of Pontic Greeks came to Greece. A 1998 submission to the UN Commission on Human Rights estimated that from 1990 to 1996 alone about 180,000 arrived from Russia, Kazakhstan and Georgia. Many spoke Russian or Pontic better than Standard Modern Greek, held qualifications that Greece did not recognise, and found integration hard; they were often settled in Thrace and in the western suburbs of Thessaloniki and Athens. They were called *Ρωσοπόντιοι*, “Russian Pontics”, a label they themselves often dislike, since they see themselves simply as Pontic Greeks returning to the homeland of their nation.",
